<p>Hello there! I was just a little too late to catch you in IRC, so I'll just leave a crit here instead.</p>
<p>Alright.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'll start with the <strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Plain and simple, I'm down for it. It's a little too typical fantasy for my liking, but the idea of this taking place in modern times (ppsst I like urban fantasy) does soften my opinion up a tad.</p>
<p>A noob wizard being held by the Foundation is pretty funny to me.</p>
<p>Sadly, the <strong>Central Narrative</strong> disappoints a good deal.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>not only does the article highlight the foundations struggle to contain a young boy who can take down walls with chalk a few words and the strand of hair</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If he's able to take down walls that easily, then is he a noob or not?</p>
<p>Personally, and this is just my hot take, I think it would be pretty entertaining if you made this guy a very, very bad mage who tries to do magical stuff and has a basic understanding, but due to his noobishness, always messes up hard and gets in a world of trouble.</p>
<p>I'd honestly love to see that; a noobish wizard in a modern, contemporary setting trying to do magic and failing miserably.</p>
<p>Though this is a pretty radical suggestion and you're free to take the story in a different method if you want.</p>
<p>If you need more crit, reach out to some other reviewers in the <a href="/butterfly-squad-roster">+Butterfly Squad</a>.</p>
<p>Anywho, I hope I helped and I bid you a good day!</p>
<p><strong>P.S.</strong></p>
<p>Almost forgot. The cat companion? Eehhh I'm personally not keen on that just because it would feel generic, but that's up to you. Animal companions for mages, I feel, is a very overdone trope. But any trope can be overcome with good enough execution. Anywho, good day, partner.</p>
<p><strong>P.P.S.</strong></p>
<p>You put in the Central Narrative twice. If you want to conserve space, I recommend using</p>
<ul>
<li>Bullet points</li>
<li>They help keep things organized</li>
<li>AND they help condense information</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
