			<div class="content" id="post-content-4831717">
									

<p>Responding to request via PM:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I will admit, I think the elevator pitch has some charm to it, and based on that summary think your idea certainly has potential.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>not only does the article highlight the foundations struggle to contain an scp that has a hard time performing even the most minor tasks without getting magic involved, failing at said magic and accidentally bringing down the nearest wall on top of him (when he was trying to make his bed make itself). while still staying kosher with the ethics department.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, this is where you begin to lose me. If this kid is such a liability when he is awake, why does the Foundation not put him into a medically induced coma, and wake him up when needed. There is certainly precedent for that in articles across the wiki, and given the alternative is the kid <strong>bringing down walls trying to make his bed</strong> it's hard for you to really justify the Foundation just letting him hang in a standard containment cell. It's one thing if you have the Foundation getting bamboozled while acting in a competent manner, but a huge turn-off for me as a reader if you portray them acting like idiots to highlight how cool your skip is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The scp’s complete immaturity and lack of expertise or knowledge of just how powerful he can be, make him extremely hard to utilize in any situation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>More reason to just slap him into a coma and raise him when needed. The Foundation should not suffer fools gladly.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I am extremely worried about this scp being to overpowered or lacking in restriction.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then add restrictions. Show that the Foundation is not just catering to a teenager to keep on its good side.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>All in all you have a solid premise, but your execution leaves much to be desired. Have the Foundation step up and not handle something this dangerous with kids gloves and you'll be in a much better space.</p>
<p>Best of luck.</p>

							</div>

								
					
