<blockquote>
<p>A modern day architect can’t explain exactly how the great pyramids were built, but can gain a basic understanding by examining them, and probably come up with a Rough theory. This scp can do the same with magical/ mythical items.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So like&#8230;is the anomaly here 'the SCP has anomalous knowledge'? Cos this just feels like the SCP is effectively a reality bender and so like&#8230;not necessarily something particularly of interest here? Like even to the Foundation, it feels like if they can't control him, they could literally just find a different reality bender, y'know?</p>
<hr />
<p>My biggest concern with this is that the narrative feels&#8230;very passive? There's not a lot driving it forwards since the SCP just kinda gets better at doing magic stuff and the Foundation respond by locking them up more and it feels like it really needs to be tied into the whole angle of the foundation utilising it more: like, to really explore the interplay between 'we keep showing him new magic so we can figure out what's happening' and 'every time we do this, he learns how to do this'; with like&#8230;the Foundation openly considering ways around this like 'what if we amnesticise him'.</p>
<p>It also feels like it doesn't really end up going somewhere: it keeps escalating until it reaches a point where it just kinda&#8230;stops without there being any kinda&#8230;big event or real sense of closure.</p>
</div>
